# Feature work → documentation sync

When you **create or materially update an app feature** (API, UI flows, integrations, env/setup, business rules, or data model), finish the implementation first, then run a **documentation pass via a subagent** before you treat the task as done.

Skip this pass for trivial fixes (typos, pure refactors with no behavior change, tests-only, or styling-only tweaks).

## Subagent workflow

1. Launch one **`generalPurpose`** subagent (`run_in_background: false`) after the feature changes are in place.
2. Give it:
   - A short summary of what changed (files and behavior).
   - This checklist of doc sources (update only what is affected):
     - `docs/PROJECT.md` — product memory, flows, glossary, status
     - `docs/BACKEND.md` — API and backend behavior
     - `docs/PRD.md` — requirements when scope or acceptance criteria shifted
     - Integration guides under `docs/` (e.g. LeadConduit, Integrity, Boberdoo notes)
     - Root `README.md` — setup, commands, env vars
   - Instruction: **decide** whether docs are stale; if **no**, report “no doc updates needed” with one-line rationale; if **yes**, **edit the relevant files** (accurate, minimal diffs, match existing tone/language), then list what was updated.

3. Incorporate the subagent’s edits into the same task. If it updated docs, include those files in your final summary for the user.

## Parent agent responsibilities

- Do **not** skip the subagent because docs “might be fine”—delegate the check every time the feature threshold above applies.
- If the subagent finds nothing to change, no doc commit is required beyond your normal feature commit.
- Prefer one doc-sync subagent per feature task; avoid parallel doc agents on the same files.
